From: tokenscope - https://github.com/PS12007/tokenscope
Subject: [experiment arm, do not merge] OpenMP n_threads == 1: apply thread priority like every other path

This is not a proposal. It is the treatment arm for FINDINGS P51.9/P51.10,
and it exists to be measured and then reverted.

What it tests. `ggml_thread_apply_priority()` is what opts a compute thread out
of Windows power throttling (EcoQoS). The ggml threadpool path calls it for
the main thread unconditionally (ggml_threadpool_new_impl), and the OpenMP
path calls it for every thread inside `#pragma omp parallel` -- but only when
n_threads > 1. An OpenMP build asked for one thread goes straight to
ggml_graph_compute_thread and never makes the call. F51's sweep found both
OpenMP builds (MSVC/vcomp and GCC/libgomp) bimodal at -t 1 on this machine,
17 +- 6 and 10 +- 4 tok/s, while both threadpool builds held ~21 +- 0.2.

What this does. Adds that one call to the n_threads == 1 branch. Nothing
changes for n_threads > 1, so -t 8 is the control.

Apply on top of 01+02, measure, and revert with the inverse of THIS file only
(it carries one hunk and nothing from 01, so -R cannot strip tokenscope):

    git -C ../llama.cpp apply ../tokenscope/patches/07-omp-single-thread-prio.patch
    ... build the arm ...
    git -C ../llama.cpp apply -R ../tokenscope/patches/07-omp-single-thread-prio.patch

then re-run the cold-start checklist (9 entries, `nth * 4` twice).

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -3444,6 +3444,7 @@ enum ggml_status ggml_graph_compute(struct ggml_cgraph * cgraph, struct ggml_cplan * cplan) {
             ggml_graph_compute_thread(&threadpool->workers[ith]);
         }
     } else {
+        ggml_thread_apply_priority(threadpool->prio);
         atomic_store_explicit(&threadpool->n_graph, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
         ggml_graph_compute_thread(&threadpool->workers[0]);
     }
